{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 42, :comments ["<b>The tzirzah</b> — according to Rashi's opinion, it is a species of locust. If so, it would have been appropriate to join it with the verse of the locust and the worm (Deuteronomy 28:38–39). It appears more likely that צְלָצַל is a name for the enemy camp. He said (Deuteronomy 28:41), \"You shall beget sons and daughters\" — but they will not be yours to serve you and sustain you in your old age, for they will go into captivity before the enemy. And He said, \"All your trees and the fruit of your land\" — which will not bear produce for you; as He mentioned, the enemy who comes upon you shall inherit them, for in the year they come upon you they will give their wealth to them and they will eat it. And the host is called צְלָצַל because they produce the sound of battle in the camp and the sound of the shofar and the roar of officers and the battle cry — from the language of צִלְצְלֵי שָׁמַע וְצִלְצְלֵי תְרוּעָה (Psalms 150:5), and וּבִמְנַעַנְעִים וּבְצֶלְצֶלִים (II Samuel 6:5), and likewise אֶרֶץ צִלְצַל כְּנָפָיִם (Isaiah 18:1) — a land that emits the whirring of wings that go far away as the eagle soars. And in the Gemara (Bava Kamma 116a), they took it as referring to מַסִּיקִין (those who press hard): he who teaches מַסִּיקִין does not err, for it is written יְיָרֵשׁ הַצְּלָצַל, and they translate it as יַחְסְנִנֵּהּ סַקָּאָה — \"the Sakaean shall inherit it.\" And he who teaches מְצִיקִים (those who oppress) does not err, for it is written בְּמָצוֹר וּבְמָצוֹק, for among them it is a term for those who bring a man into siege and distress. And know that the reproofs concerning the blight and the mildew and the blasting and the pestilence and the tree and the fruit of the ground and all the other reproofs are all \"until He has consumed you from upon the land which you are going there to possess it.\" But after the exile, He does not curse them except to serve there other elohim, wood and stone. Yet sometimes He returns to reprove them in the land, as He said (Deuteronomy 28:36), \"HaShem will lead you and your king whom you set over you to a nation you have not known, you and your fathers, and you shall serve there other elohim\" — and this is the exile that we went into to Rome upon the journey of King Agrippa there. And afterwards He returns and says (Deuteronomy 28:38), \"You shall bring out much seed to the field,\" and so on — and these are reproofs while they are in the land, for so He says after it (Deuteronomy 28:63), \"And you shall be plucked up from upon the land,\" which is the exile. And in my opinion this too is among His hints: He said, \"HaShem will lead you and your king whom you set over you to a nation you have not known,\" upon the journey of King Agrippa to Rome. It is also possible that He hints at another king before Agrippa, namely Aristobulus, whom a Roman military commander seized and led there in chains, and they became a byword and a taunt among all the peoples, who marveled at their valor — how mighty warriors fell and their weapons of war perished, as never before. And afterwards Agrippa came a second time with an emissary of the king of Rome in the land, and conquered very great strongholds in the land of Judah. And then was fulfilled what He said (Deuteronomy 28:48), \"And you shall serve your enemies whom HaShem will send against you, in hunger and thirst,\" and so on. And He said, \"HaShem will bring against you a nation from afar\" (Deuteronomy 28:49), for Vespasian and his son Titus came with a great army of Romans to the land, and they captured all the fortified cities of Judah and oppressed them severely, as is known from the books — that they also captured the walls of Jerusalem, and nothing remained except the Temple and the Temple Mount wall, and they ate the flesh of their sons and daughters. And when it too was captured, then was fulfilled \"And you shall be plucked up from upon the land,\" and then the Romans returned to their land, and through their hand came the exile of Jerusalem, and through the hand of many peoples who were with them — from Greece, Egypt, Aram, and many other peoples. And there was fulfilled (Deuteronomy 28:64), \"And HaShem will scatter you among all the peoples,\" and so on. And the verses that follow thereafter speak of their punishment in exile, and likewise \"And your life shall hang in doubt before you\" (Deuteronomy 28:66), because of the terror we experience in exile at the hands of the peoples who decree decrees against us continually. And the correct interpretation is that it alludes to those generations who lived at the time of the Destruction of the Second Temple, for they sought to destroy them completely; therefore He said after it (Deuteronomy 28:68), \"And HaShem will bring you back to Egypt in ships,\" which happened when they resolved to expel them from the land. And our Rabbis said (Esther Rabbah, Proem), why to Egypt? Because it is as ugly and evil for a slave when he returns to his first master. If so, He mentioned Egypt by way of reproof. But after we are in exile in the lands of our enemies, the work of our hands has not been cursed, nor our flocks and herds, nor our vineyards and olive groves and what is sown in the field; rather, we are in those lands like the other inhabitants of that land or better than them, in His mercy toward us, for our dwelling in exile is under the assurance He gave us (Leviticus 26:44), \"And yet for all that, when they are in the land of their enemies, I will not reject them, neither will I abhor them, to destroy them, to break My covenant with them, for I am HaShem their Elohim.\" And I have already explained in the section of Bechukotai (Leviticus 26:16) the secret of this covenant, and that it applies to the time of our exile today at the hand of the fourth beast, and afterwards He promises redemption from it.\n\n<b>Now</b> we have examined the punishments for idol worship. And they are mentioned in the Torah in many places. Among them is what is said at the beginning: \"When you beget sons and sons' sons, and you grow old in the land, and you deal corruptly, and make a graven image,\" and so on. And this is a warning to Israel: if they worship idolatry after standing in the land for many days and becoming settled in it — the punishment for this is \"I call heaven and earth to witness against you this day,\" until \"and HaShem will scatter you among the peoples, and you shall remain few in number.\" And there He said, \"And you shall serve there elohim, the work of men's hands.\" And He promised, \"But from there you shall seek HaShem your Elohim, and you shall find Him,\" and so on. And this was fulfilled among them, as I have mentioned and explained already. And in another section He said, \"And it shall be when HaShem your Elohim brings you into the land which He swore to your fathers,\" and so on — \"You shall not go after other elohim, of the elohim of the peoples who are around you.\" And the punishment: \"For HaShem your Elohim is a jealous El in your midst, lest the anger of HaShem your Elohim blaze against you and destroy you from upon the face of the earth.\" This is a warning to those who enter the land: if they immediately cling to idolatry and forget the El who brought them out of Egypt, turning after idolatry. And this Israel did not do, and this punishment did not come upon them. And the language of the verse indicates this — that it is a warning; it did not allude to anything destined to come, as He said, \"Lest the anger of HaShem your Elohim blaze against you and destroy you\" — meaning, you have cause to fear this.\n\nA third section: \"Take heed to yourself lest you forget HaShem your Elohim, in not keeping His commandments and His ordinances and His statutes,\" and so on. And He said, \"And your heart be lifted up, and you forget HaShem your Elohim, who brought you out of the land of Egypt, who led you through the great and terrible wilderness\" — the entire section. \"And you say in your heart: My power and the might of my hand has gotten me this wealth. But you shall remember HaShem your Elohim, for it is He who gives you power to get wealth.\" And He said there, \"And it shall be, if you do forget HaShem your Elohim, and go after other elohim and serve them and bow down to them — I testify against you this day.\" And this too is a warning to those who enter the land: if they forget the honorable Name and deny that He brought them out of the land of Egypt and did nothing for them, but rather by their own power they did everything, for they are mighty; and therefore they worship idolatry as they please. And the punishment for this: \"You shall surely perish\" — \"like the nations that HaShem destroys before you, so you shall perish.\" And the language of the verse indicates that He warns them lest they do so when they come to the land, before they are settled in it — before He has destroyed all the peoples in the land. Therefore He said, \"Like the nations that HaShem destroys before you\" — which was at the time of the destruction of the peoples. And He warns that He would withdraw His hand from destroying the peoples and destroy Israel like them. And this too Israel did not do, and such did not come upon them.\n\nAnd in the fourth instance He warned and specified and said, \"And it shall be, if you do not hearken to the voice of HaShem your Elohim, to observe to do all His commandments and His statutes, all these curses shall come upon you,\" and so on. And all the words of the covenant, and a great promise at the end: \"And it shall be, when all these things come upon you, and you take them to heart, and you return to HaShem your Elohim, and HaShem your Elohim will return your captivity and have compassion on you,\" and the conclusion of the section. And this is a warning and an allusion to this exile of ours and to the complete redemption through which we shall be redeemed from it.\n\nAnd the fifth section: a warning in the manner of fulfilled prophecy. \"And HaShem said to Moses: Behold, you are about to lie down with your fathers, and this people will rise up,\" and so on, \"and this song shall testify before them as a witness, for it shall not be forgotten from the mouth of their seed,\" and so on. And this is the warning that was fulfilled in its entirety according to its plain sense and meaning."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {0 "The comment weaves together multiple discussions: the meaning of צְלָצַל in v. 42, the structure and chronology of the curses in Deuteronomy 28, and a survey of the Torah's five passages warning against idolatry. The paragraph break marked by וְהִנֵּה transitions from the curse-chronology argument to the idolatry-warning survey; it has been preserved as a paragraph break within the same entry. 'Fourth beast' in the kabbalistic reference to the exile is rendered literally from חַיָּה הָרְבִיעִית without further decoding."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:58:32.422272157Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}